Four notes on what the enumeration does and does not cover, so the completeness
claim is exact rather than broad:

  * IT IS THE .NET GRAPH OF THIS SOLUTION AT THE PINNED VERSIONS. Changing a
    pin, adding a package, or restoring on a different SDK feature band can
    change the graph, so this block is accurate for the pins in
    Directory.Packages.props and must be regenerated with the command above
    whenever one of them moves. That is the same discipline the direct list
    above already carries.
  * PACKAGES THAT RESOLVE TO THE SHARED FRAMEWORK ARE NOT PACKAGES HERE. On
    net10.0 several declared dependencies are satisfied by Microsoft.NETCore.App
    or Microsoft.AspNetCore.App rather than by a package. They appear in no lock
    file, contribute no third-party terms, and therefore appear in no line above.
    The two shared frameworks themselves are Microsoft components of the .NET
    platform, licensed MIT. Microsoft.OpenApi.YamlReader is the clearest worked
    example: it declares exactly three dependencies -- Microsoft.OpenApi 2.12.0,
    which is already a direct reference above; SharpYaml 2.1.4, which is the one
    package it genuinely adds and is enumerated above; and System.Text.Json
    8.0.5, which resolves to the shared framework and so appears nowhere.
  * IT IS NOT A CONTAINER IMAGE INVENTORY. The base images named in each
    service's Dockerfile carry their own operating-system package sets, and
    nothing in this file enumerates those. An image-level inventory is a separate
    artifact and none is produced in this phase.
  * IT IS NOT AN npm GRAPH. The three npm devDependencies below are direct
